WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:04.160
So moving on from those kind of fundamental components

2
00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.160
onto something a little bit more content-focused,

3
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.339
let's take a look at animation.

4
00:00:08.339 --> 00:00:11.460
Animation is made up of a kind of a set of different assets

5
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.699
to piece the whole system together in Unreal.

6
00:00:14.699 --> 00:00:17.179
We have skeleton assets, skeletal meshes, physics

7
00:00:17.179 --> 00:00:20.859
assets, animation sequences, anim montages, animation

8
00:00:20.859 --> 00:00:22.660
instance, or otherwise referred

9
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.300
to as animation blueprint.

10
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.579
And lastly, anim notifies, which actually

11
00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:29.019
isn't represented by an asset in the content browser,

12
00:00:29.019 --> 00:00:30.859
but is still a pretty important concept

13
00:00:30.859 --> 00:00:32.380
to get your head around.

14
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.140
So we'll start off with skeleton assets.

15
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.820
The term skeleton is probably something

16
00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.460
that most of us are used to when we're

17
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.619
talking about animations within the context of video games.

18
00:00:40.619 --> 00:00:43.420
It's a hierarchical framework of bones

19
00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.539
that are linked together and can be

20
00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:48.460
used to define complex motions.

21
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:52.020
In Unreal, these are associated with the animation data.

22
00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.060
So a skeleton asset has the bones in it, the bone data,

23
00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.140
but also is associated tightly with the animations that

24
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:00.659
are for that skeleton.

25
00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:05.099
The skeleton asset can be shared by several skeletal meshes,

26
00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:07.659
and we don't always require exact matches,

27
00:01:07.659 --> 00:01:10.939
meaning we can have meshes that are missing certain bones

28
00:01:10.939 --> 00:01:14.900
and the animations still function.

29
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.939
So the elements that the asset is

30
00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:19.379
kind of responsible for animation retargeting,

31
00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:21.180
which we already touched a little bit on, the idea

32
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.180
that characters that have the same skeleton,

33
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.939
but might have slightly different portions and things

34
00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:27.900
like that, that can be done utilizing the skeleton asset.

35
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.099
The skeleton asset also stores sockets.

36
00:01:30.099 --> 00:01:32.260
Sockets are a really important gameplay concept

37
00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:35.299
where you might not necessarily have a bone in the middle

38
00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:36.819
of your character's hand, but you often

39
00:01:36.819 --> 00:01:38.459
want to be able to attach items there,

40
00:01:38.459 --> 00:01:41.180
such as guns or pickups they're holding or torches

41
00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.379
and that kind of thing.

42
00:01:42.379 --> 00:01:44.379
And so sockets is a kind of additional layer

43
00:01:44.379 --> 00:01:47.780
that lets us add game accessible,

44
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:50.019
something like bones onto our skeleton.

45
00:01:50.019 --> 00:01:52.299
And those are stored in the skeleton asset.

46
00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:54.340
They also store animation notifies,

47
00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.420
which are ways for animations to communicate to gameplay code

48
00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:59.900
to fire off certain events.

49
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:01.579
We have animation curves.

50
00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:06.739
Animation curve data is like an additional set of curves

51
00:02:06.739 --> 00:02:08.419
that we can use to drive certain properties,

52
00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:14.979
for example, material blends or things like morph targets.

53
00:02:14.979 --> 00:02:18.020
And lastly, we have animation slot names.

54
00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:21.539
So slot names are used on an animated character

55
00:02:21.539 --> 00:02:26.500
to define kind of channels for animation montages to play in.

56
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.780
This is really important when you want certain montages

57
00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.819
to only be in play at certain times

58
00:02:31.819 --> 00:02:33.939
and maybe to only affect certain parts of the character.

59
00:02:33.939 --> 00:02:37.439
Slot names become very, very powerful there.

60
00:02:37.439 --> 00:02:39.520
So the skeletal mesh actually represents

61
00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.840
the kind of visual mesh for our animated character.

62
00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.280
So it's actually the surface data.

63
00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:48.639
And we've highlighted both SKM Mani and SKM Quinn here.

64
00:02:48.639 --> 00:02:51.759
SKM being the kind of standard nomenclature

65
00:02:51.759 --> 00:02:53.919
for the kind of prefix before a skeletal mesh,

66
00:02:53.919 --> 00:02:56.360
hence the name asset.

67
00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.080
And those vertices are essentially tied to our bones.

68
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.240
But the point being that both of these characters

69
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:03.639
would share the same skeleton.

70
00:03:03.639 --> 00:03:04.599
And this is what we're talking about,

71
00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:06.840
the idea that by doing that,

72
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.120
by having these skeletal meshes

73
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.599
both share the same skeleton,

74
00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:11.560
it means we can recycle animations

75
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:13.199
and we can reuse the same animations

76
00:03:13.199 --> 00:03:16.560
on different looking characters.

77
00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.039
The skeletal mesh itself actually holds a reference

78
00:03:19.039 --> 00:03:21.159
to the skeleton that it is driven by.

79
00:03:21.159 --> 00:03:23.159
And skeletal meshes are also responsible

80
00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:25.159
for the kind of lodding of the visuals.

81
00:03:25.159 --> 00:03:28.240
So lodding is probably a term we're all familiar with,

82
00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:29.319
level of detail.

83
00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:31.199
As you get further away from this asset,

84
00:03:31.199 --> 00:03:34.360
obviously it's going to kind of reduce infidelity

85
00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.199
because we don't need 4K textures

86
00:03:36.199 --> 00:03:37.639
when something's a mile away.

87
00:03:37.639 --> 00:03:39.199
So it's actually the skeletal mesh

88
00:03:39.199 --> 00:03:41.960
that is handling that lodding.

89
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.159
It's important to highlight as well

90
00:03:43.159 --> 00:03:48.159
that because even though we might play animations

91
00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:49.439
on our skeletal mesh,

92
00:03:49.439 --> 00:03:51.639
that's actually what animations are suitable

93
00:03:51.639 --> 00:03:52.800
for a skeletal mesh,

94
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.719
because animations are actually driven

95
00:03:54.719 --> 00:03:57.319
by the skeleton and are driving the skeleton.

96
00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:00.199
What animations are suitable for the skeletal mesh

97
00:04:00.199 --> 00:04:04.750
is actually defined by a skeleton asset.

98
00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:06.669
Then we have the physics asset.

99
00:04:06.669 --> 00:04:09.629
This is essentially a simplified representation

100
00:04:09.629 --> 00:04:11.550
of our skeletal mesh.

101
00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.909
And this is kind of an unreal concept.

102
00:04:13.909 --> 00:04:15.349
It's not a kind of broad thing

103
00:04:15.349 --> 00:04:16.310
that you would see necessarily

104
00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.389
in other pieces of software,

105
00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:19.910
but it's how we represent

106
00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:23.829
very often the collision of a skeletal mesh.

107
00:04:24.709 --> 00:04:28.029
So I'll very, very quickly demonstrate this.

108
00:04:28.029 --> 00:04:30.829
If we get our project open,

109
00:04:30.829 --> 00:04:32.269
we've actually got,

110
00:04:32.269 --> 00:04:41.019
if we jump to this, we can see,

111
00:04:41.019 --> 00:04:42.779
if we jump inside our physics asset,

112
00:04:42.779 --> 00:04:46.339
you can see we have these kind of like a set of balls

113
00:04:46.339 --> 00:04:47.699
or kind of like various shapes.

114
00:04:47.699 --> 00:04:51.019
It's quite often capsules and spheres or cubes.

115
00:04:51.019 --> 00:04:52.779
And you can see we have a representation

116
00:04:52.779 --> 00:04:55.860
of our kind of character skeleton here on the left.

117
00:04:55.899 --> 00:04:59.180
And under each skeleton, under each bone,

118
00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:00.699
we can see the elements

119
00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:02.980
that are actually like tied to these bones

120
00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.899
and what bones are attached to those bones

121
00:05:04.899 --> 00:05:08.660
and what primitives are attached to each of these

122
00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:10.139
to represent that mesh.

123
00:05:10.139 --> 00:05:12.779
So if you look here over here in the details panel,

124
00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:14.579
we can see in this body setup,

125
00:05:14.579 --> 00:05:16.300
we've got a list of primitives.

126
00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.819
And on the clavicle,

127
00:05:17.819 --> 00:05:20.620
we have one capsule that represents,

128
00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.740
the kind of, that we're kind of vaguely using

129
00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:25.379
to map in a very, very simplified way

130
00:05:25.939 --> 00:05:28.379
what our kind of skeletal mesh looks like.

131
00:05:28.379 --> 00:05:30.779
And again, I keep using the term skeletal mesh here

132
00:05:30.779 --> 00:05:33.180
rather than the actual skeleton

133
00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.339
because it's the skeletal mesh

134
00:05:35.339 --> 00:05:37.060
that our physics asset is tied to.

135
00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:38.899
That's because even two characters

136
00:05:38.899 --> 00:05:40.860
that have the same skeleton,

137
00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:42.740
they might have very, very different proportions.

138
00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.939
They might have very, very different geometry.

139
00:05:44.939 --> 00:05:47.100
So it's the skeletal mesh

140
00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.500
that the physics asset is actually trying

141
00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:52.420
to give us a simplified representation of.

142
00:05:52.459 --> 00:05:55.540
The kind of obvious example when we would need this

143
00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:57.939
is anything kind of collision related.

144
00:05:57.939 --> 00:06:00.300
So that might be line traces, overlaps,

145
00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:01.939
actual collision itself,

146
00:06:01.939 --> 00:06:04.019
physics simulation, cloth simulation,

147
00:06:04.019 --> 00:06:07.579
anything like that where the actual skeletal mesh

148
00:06:07.579 --> 00:06:09.540
might have way too many vertices

149
00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:11.300
to check against to be efficient.

150
00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.819
We can use this for a kind of simplified version

151
00:06:13.819 --> 00:06:14.740
of that collision.

152
00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.029
Yes, the reference skeleton

153
00:06:19.029 --> 00:06:20.829
is paired with the actual skeletal mesh.

154
00:06:20.829 --> 00:06:21.990
And the last thing to mention

155
00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:25.389
is that when you have a blueprint

156
00:06:25.389 --> 00:06:29.509
that is using any skeletal mesh,

157
00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:32.069
you can actually override the physics asset

158
00:06:32.069 --> 00:06:33.509
that is used by that blueprint.

159
00:06:33.509 --> 00:06:34.949
So even though the physics asset

160
00:06:34.949 --> 00:06:36.629
is tied to the skeletal mesh,

161
00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:37.870
at the component level,

162
00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.110
at the skeletal mesh component,

163
00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.029
you can actually override the physics asset

164
00:06:42.029 --> 00:06:45.540
that it's using at runtime.

165
00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.980
Animation sequences are essentially

166
00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:51.060
what the actual asset in Unreal

167
00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.019
that represents an animation.

168
00:06:53.019 --> 00:06:55.540
They're the kind of building block that we use

169
00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:58.220
to play animations either through the Animation Blueprint

170
00:06:58.220 --> 00:06:59.579
or on their own.

171
00:06:59.579 --> 00:07:01.579
You can play,

172
00:07:01.579 --> 00:07:04.139
without the concept of an Animation Blueprint,

173
00:07:04.139 --> 00:07:06.740
you can actually just play an animation on its own.

174
00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:07.980
It's less common

175
00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:10.300
because it's much, much more restrictive.

176
00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:12.779
The vast majority of the time we're doing,

177
00:07:12.779 --> 00:07:14.060
certainly for character animation,

178
00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.100
we need a little bit more complexity out of it.

179
00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.540
But in the context that we just kind of need

180
00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:19.220
a single fire and forget animation,

181
00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:22.379
you can have just a single animations playing.

182
00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:24.100
So animations are made up of key frames.

183
00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:25.740
This is probably concepts

184
00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:27.300
that most people are relatively familiar with.

185
00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:29.139
It's not an Unreal specific thing.

186
00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:31.300
But the idea being that you have

187
00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.420
an animation might last six seconds.

188
00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:34.740
And along that time,

189
00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:36.420
you would key frame bones

190
00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:38.699
to be in different positions and rotations,

191
00:07:38.699 --> 00:07:41.339
and then the vertices that those bones are tied to

192
00:07:41.339 --> 00:07:42.779
are gonna drive the mesh

193
00:07:42.779 --> 00:07:45.019
that that skeleton is tied to.

194
00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:47.740
As we said before,

195
00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:50.699
animations are tied to specific skeleton assets,

196
00:07:50.699 --> 00:07:52.300
not the skeletal mesh itself.

197
00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:56.740
And animation sequences can be played through code,

198
00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:58.699
but more typically,

199
00:07:58.699 --> 00:08:01.100
they're utilized through the animation blueprint.

200
00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:02.699
Again, we touched on it a little bit

201
00:08:02.699 --> 00:08:05.819
in the skeleton asset,

202
00:08:05.819 --> 00:08:09.339
but whilst the skeleton asset stores the curves,

203
00:08:09.339 --> 00:08:11.459
they would obviously be used and driven

204
00:08:11.459 --> 00:08:12.579
in the actual animation.

205
00:08:12.579 --> 00:08:14.620
So it's our animation sequences

206
00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:16.220
that would drive that curve data

207
00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:19.379
that we might use for things like material parameters

208
00:08:19.379 --> 00:08:21.740
or morph targets.

209
00:08:21.779 --> 00:08:24.860
We can actually also use root motion in animation.

210
00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:26.540
So root motion for the term

211
00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:27.939
for people who aren't familiar with that

212
00:08:27.939 --> 00:08:30.620
is the idea that we can affect the position of our root

213
00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:35.620
of our entities actual position with our animation.

214
00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:39.379
So we read what changes the animation have made

215
00:08:39.379 --> 00:08:42.460
to the root of that skeleton.

216
00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:45.500
And we interpret that as actual movement of the character.

217
00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:48.100
So you can do that through animation sequences,

218
00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:50.100
but in the context that your game

219
00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:51.740
is networked multiplayer,

220
00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:53.139
doing it through an animation sequence

221
00:08:53.139 --> 00:08:54.019
isn't gonna be suitable.

222
00:08:54.019 --> 00:08:57.539
You would need to move to animation montages,

223
00:08:57.539 --> 00:09:00.860
which brings us conveniently onto animation montages.

224
00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:03.620
Animation montages are the kind of step up

225
00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:05.899
in complexity from animation sequences.

226
00:09:05.899 --> 00:09:09.100
They give us a way to play animation

227
00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:12.820
directly through blueprints or in C++.

228
00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:15.980
So whereas with a standard animation sequence,

229
00:09:16.019 --> 00:09:20.899
we can assign an animation to a skeleton for that to play,

230
00:09:20.899 --> 00:09:22.659
it's more typical that when we're trying

231
00:09:22.659 --> 00:09:24.820
to play animations at runtime,

232
00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:27.539
even if that's possibly simultaneous

233
00:09:27.539 --> 00:09:29.299
to something that already has an animation instance

234
00:09:29.299 --> 00:09:30.139
controlling it,

235
00:09:30.139 --> 00:09:32.100
we can do that through animation montages.

236
00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:34.580
And animation montages through the use of slots

237
00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:37.100
are actually kind of intended often to be used

238
00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:39.700
in conjunction with an animation instance

239
00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:42.539
that might be controlling it 95% of the time,

240
00:09:42.539 --> 00:09:45.620
but you're playing animation montage in certain contexts

241
00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:48.570
for certain gameplay scenarios.

242
00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:50.690
So what animation montages do,

243
00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:53.049
they do function very much like an animation,

244
00:09:53.049 --> 00:09:53.970
but they give us the ability

245
00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:56.610
to stitch several animations together.

246
00:09:56.610 --> 00:09:59.129
So if we take an example, a jump animation,

247
00:09:59.129 --> 00:10:03.799
which we've got here,

248
00:10:03.799 --> 00:10:06.360
you can see this montage is actually made up

249
00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:07.200
of several sections.

250
00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:08.799
We have a default section,

251
00:10:08.799 --> 00:10:10.480
which is essentially a jump start.

252
00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.240
We have a loop and then we have a land.

253
00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:16.240
And land plays quite no noxiously loud sound,

254
00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:19.120
but essentially this is actually made up of three animations.

255
00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:20.759
We can see we've got the jump animation,

256
00:10:20.759 --> 00:10:21.960
we've got the falling loop,

257
00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:23.759
and we've got the land animation.

258
00:10:23.759 --> 00:10:26.000
But as it's kind of already been demonstrated,

259
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.519
these sections can feed into one another.

260
00:10:28.519 --> 00:10:30.759
So by default, when you're jumping,

261
00:10:30.759 --> 00:10:32.879
we don't know how long we're necessarily going to play

262
00:10:32.879 --> 00:10:34.559
the loop animation for.

263
00:10:34.559 --> 00:10:38.440
So what the loop animation do unsurprisingly is loop.

264
00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:40.600
And we can define that in the montage itself.

265
00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:41.759
This isn't default behavior.

266
00:10:41.759 --> 00:10:44.039
We've specified here that we want the for loop

267
00:10:44.039 --> 00:10:46.200
to play itself repeatedly.

268
00:10:47.159 --> 00:10:49.639
And what we can do is then jump between these sections.

269
00:10:49.639 --> 00:10:51.639
So these names actually matter.

270
00:10:51.639 --> 00:10:53.440
With gameplay, we can essentially drive this

271
00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:55.159
and say, jump to the loop section,

272
00:10:55.159 --> 00:10:56.519
jump to the land section.

273
00:10:56.519 --> 00:10:57.559
So when our character lands,

274
00:10:57.559 --> 00:10:59.879
we would obviously jump out of the loop section

275
00:10:59.879 --> 00:11:04.509
into our land section to finish the animation off.

276
00:11:04.509 --> 00:11:06.470
So the animation instance is the last

277
00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:09.149
of these animation based assets.

278
00:11:09.149 --> 00:11:11.990
And it's also probably the most complicated.

279
00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:14.190
For a start, there's a naming convention.

280
00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:16.669
Animation instance is the term used in C++,

281
00:11:16.669 --> 00:11:17.509
content level.

282
00:11:17.509 --> 00:11:19.110
It's referred to as the animation blueprint,

283
00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:21.149
hence the prefix ABP.

284
00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:24.149
And it's essentially the point at which

285
00:11:24.149 --> 00:11:25.350
we stitch everything together

286
00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.509
when we need something more complicated.

287
00:11:27.509 --> 00:11:30.110
They're very typically used for player animations,

288
00:11:30.110 --> 00:11:31.750
anything where we would need

289
00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:33.190
several different animations.

290
00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:34.269
We need branching logic.

291
00:11:34.269 --> 00:11:35.750
We need many different things coming in

292
00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:37.669
and we maybe need different kind of layers

293
00:11:37.669 --> 00:11:39.549
affecting different parts of the body.

294
00:11:39.549 --> 00:11:41.350
Anything that's kind of more complex

295
00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:42.909
than fire and forget animations

296
00:11:42.909 --> 00:11:44.830
or single loops that we're performing

297
00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:47.669
would probably require an animation blueprint.

298
00:11:47.669 --> 00:11:49.990
These are broken up into kind of two key components.

299
00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:52.149
We have the event graph and the anim graph.

300
00:11:52.149 --> 00:11:53.710
And we'll go into both of those

301
00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:55.509
in more detail in just a second.

302
00:11:55.509 --> 00:11:56.509
But just before we do that,

303
00:11:56.509 --> 00:11:58.070
we're gonna move on to anim notifies

304
00:11:58.070 --> 00:11:59.309
to cover that just a little bit

305
00:11:59.309 --> 00:12:00.789
and then we'll loop back.

306
00:12:00.789 --> 00:12:03.139
No pun intended.

307
00:12:03.139 --> 00:12:05.620
So anim notifies, as mentioned earlier,

308
00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:08.139
are ways that we can essentially trigger

309
00:12:08.139 --> 00:12:11.179
certain gameplay behaviors from an animation.

310
00:12:11.179 --> 00:12:13.659
It's pretty obvious why that might be useful,

311
00:12:13.659 --> 00:12:18.379
but very often we have certain gameplay side events

312
00:12:18.379 --> 00:12:20.740
that we need to happen in sync with animation.

313
00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:23.100
For example, if our character is swinging a sword,

314
00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:24.340
they might need to grunt,

315
00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:26.860
play a sound at the right time.

316
00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:29.500
Very often games have important things

317
00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:32.700
like collision or attacks synced up

318
00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:34.220
with the animations for those things

319
00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:35.820
so that it's very clear to the player

320
00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:37.580
when these things do and don't happen.

321
00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:39.980
So we might have a certain point in that sword swing.

322
00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:42.539
We activate the collision for our swords,

323
00:12:42.539 --> 00:12:44.980
for our actual weapon so that it can deal damage.

324
00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:46.779
And then towards the end of the swing

325
00:12:46.779 --> 00:12:49.179
is when we disable it again.

326
00:12:49.179 --> 00:12:52.820
And all of that should be driven by the animation itself

327
00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:55.740
rather than us having to really intricately sync up

328
00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:56.580
the code with the animation

329
00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:58.820
and just hope that the two things line up.

330
00:12:58.820 --> 00:12:59.659
There are two ways

331
00:12:59.659 --> 00:13:02.340
that we can actually create these anim notifies.

332
00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:03.980
I was slightly misleading before

333
00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:06.379
when I said anim notifies aren't represented by an asset

334
00:13:06.379 --> 00:13:07.899
because you can make a blueprint

335
00:13:07.899 --> 00:13:09.580
that inherits from the anim notify class,

336
00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:11.019
which is what we'll look at first.

337
00:13:11.019 --> 00:13:12.220
And the second way of doing it

338
00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:15.620
is implementing custom notifies in your animations

339
00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:17.059
for your skeleton,

340
00:13:17.059 --> 00:13:18.500
which are then implemented

341
00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:20.419
on a kind of animation instance

342
00:13:20.419 --> 00:13:21.980
by animation instance basis

343
00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:24.220
for kind of more bespoke behavior.

344
00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:27.419
So let's actually kind of just look into that a little bit

345
00:13:27.419 --> 00:13:28.539
and then we'll kind of demonstrate

346
00:13:28.539 --> 00:13:31.129
how we might actually implement them.

347
00:13:31.129 --> 00:13:33.009
So first off with making anim notifies

348
00:13:33.009 --> 00:13:34.529
that inherit from the anim notify class,

349
00:13:34.529 --> 00:13:35.610
so the actual blueprints

350
00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:37.730
that you'd see in your content browser,

351
00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:39.809
we've got two types that we can author.

352
00:13:39.809 --> 00:13:42.210
We have anim notifies, just the plain one,

353
00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:44.090
and that's just a kind of single event

354
00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:46.250
that will occur in whatever point

355
00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:47.570
you specify in your animation.

356
00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:50.090
And then we have anim notify state.

357
00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:52.769
These function relatively similarly,

358
00:13:52.769 --> 00:13:55.129
but anim notify state is an anim notify

359
00:13:55.129 --> 00:13:57.129
that might have some kind of duration.

360
00:13:57.129 --> 00:13:59.649
So we have a begin, an end,

361
00:13:59.649 --> 00:14:01.769
and for every animation tick,

362
00:14:01.769 --> 00:14:03.649
and it's important to highlight animation tick,

363
00:14:03.649 --> 00:14:05.730
so it's not necessarily always in sync

364
00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:07.330
with your game's world tick,

365
00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:10.769
depends on your animations frame rate and things like that,

366
00:14:10.769 --> 00:14:12.529
then you would have received this notify tick.

367
00:14:12.529 --> 00:14:15.450
So anim notify just has a receive notify function

368
00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:16.730
that you can override.

369
00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:19.169
Anim notify state has notify begin,

370
00:14:19.169 --> 00:14:21.129
notify end, notify tick.

371
00:14:21.129 --> 00:14:23.210
Both of these events also have a function

372
00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:25.409
that you can override called get notify name.

373
00:14:25.409 --> 00:14:26.450
And this is essentially a way

374
00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:28.769
to provide a friendly name to the editor

375
00:14:28.769 --> 00:14:30.769
so that when you put these notifies

376
00:14:30.769 --> 00:14:33.049
in your animation sequence timeline,

377
00:14:33.049 --> 00:14:35.970
you can kind of see just without even having

378
00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:38.809
to go into the detail what kind of notify it is.

379
00:14:38.809 --> 00:14:40.929
Just a couple of things to mention about these functions.

380
00:14:40.929 --> 00:14:43.889
These are const, so that really massively affects

381
00:14:43.889 --> 00:14:44.889
the way you need to be thinking

382
00:14:44.889 --> 00:14:46.370
about what you can do in an anim notify.

383
00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:49.250
You can't edit state of the notify itself.

384
00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:51.769
You can't store variables that you use for later,

385
00:14:51.769 --> 00:14:53.210
which is obviously particularly relevant

386
00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:55.970
for anim notify state where you might instantly think,

387
00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:59.370
oh, I will fire off some events

388
00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:00.610
that will tick frequently,

389
00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:03.289
like a kind of damage timer,

390
00:15:03.289 --> 00:15:06.529
or I'll have some kind of countdown or something like that.

391
00:15:06.529 --> 00:15:08.730
Unfortunately, that's not completely suitable

392
00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:10.649
within the anim notify itself

393
00:15:10.649 --> 00:15:13.289
because these functions are const.

394
00:15:13.289 --> 00:15:15.970
But as you can see, quite a lot of information

395
00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:17.409
is provided through the function.

396
00:15:17.409 --> 00:15:19.289
So we have the mesh component

397
00:15:19.289 --> 00:15:21.250
that the anim notify is actually being run on.

398
00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:23.210
You have the animation that is being fired,

399
00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:25.690
and then you just have some additional data

400
00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:27.769
about the actual event itself.

401
00:15:27.769 --> 00:15:30.009
And for the anim notify state,

402
00:15:30.009 --> 00:15:32.889
we also get an additional couple of variables.

403
00:15:32.889 --> 00:15:34.330
We have the duration.

404
00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:36.090
So when our notify begin,

405
00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:38.690
we have the total duration this event is going to take.

406
00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:42.529
And on our tick, we have the kind of tick duration again

407
00:15:42.529 --> 00:15:45.090
of the since the last animation frame.

408
00:15:46.049 --> 00:15:47.850
Just to kind of quickly actually demonstrate

409
00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:50.490
these being used rather than just talking in abstracts,

410
00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:52.889
let's jump into the project

411
00:15:52.889 --> 00:15:54.809
and take a quick look at some ways

412
00:15:54.809 --> 00:15:57.659
in which we've actually implemented these.

413
00:15:57.659 --> 00:16:01.860
So if we go to our content browser and we find notifies,

414
00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:04.019
these are just two example notifies that I've made.

415
00:16:04.019 --> 00:16:06.059
So these aren't built into Unreal.

416
00:16:06.059 --> 00:16:10.039
These are managed custom ones that we've made.

417
00:16:10.360 --> 00:16:12.840
And this is an example of just our plain notify.

418
00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:15.200
This is quite a typical thing that you might see.

419
00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:17.639
And this is a notify to represent a footfall.

420
00:16:17.639 --> 00:16:20.600
And because obviously we can't fully author

421
00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:25.120
just playing a sound in a walking animations or whatever,

422
00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:27.279
because it depends on what kind of surface

423
00:16:27.279 --> 00:16:28.360
you're standing on.

424
00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:30.679
What we do instead is we,

425
00:16:31.679 --> 00:16:34.480
this notify has like a ball built into it.

426
00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:36.000
And you can actually demonstrate

427
00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:39.159
actually using this unnotifying animation

428
00:16:39.159 --> 00:16:41.639
to kind of show how that's made available to us,

429
00:16:41.639 --> 00:16:43.840
but we can choose which foot it is.

430
00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:45.519
And then based on which foot it is,

431
00:16:45.519 --> 00:16:47.960
we get a socket from our skeleton,

432
00:16:47.960 --> 00:16:49.440
which we talked about before.

433
00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:52.360
We trace downwards to the floor on the visibility channel.

434
00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:53.919
And if we hit something,

435
00:16:53.919 --> 00:16:56.679
then we check what kind of physical surface it is.

436
00:16:56.679 --> 00:16:58.240
And then we play a different sound

437
00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:00.200
based on what kind of surface.

438
00:17:01.419 --> 00:17:03.419
So that just lets us do like a ray chase,

439
00:17:03.419 --> 00:17:05.500
check against the physics type of the floor.

440
00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:07.259
And then that way, if we're walking on concrete,

441
00:17:07.259 --> 00:17:09.259
we can play a concrete footstep sound effect.

442
00:17:09.259 --> 00:17:10.259
If we're walking on dirt,

443
00:17:10.259 --> 00:17:14.220
we can play a dirt foot for sound effect.

444
00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:16.779
An example of the animation and notify state

445
00:17:16.779 --> 00:17:20.380
might just be a spin attack or something.

446
00:17:21.339 --> 00:17:22.380
I couldn't immediately think

447
00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:24.579
of such a fantastic implementation here,

448
00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:26.940
but yeah, essentially what I'm doing

449
00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:29.299
is just on our notify tick,

450
00:17:29.299 --> 00:17:32.700
I'm doing a sphere overlap with every object around us,

451
00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:35.140
other than the owner of our mesh component,

452
00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:36.779
so essentially ourselves.

453
00:17:36.779 --> 00:17:39.940
And based on this damage radius that we can specify,

454
00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:42.859
we're applying damage to every actor

455
00:17:42.859 --> 00:17:45.940
with a damage per second value that we can specify.

456
00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:48.740
Because it's const, we're not changing this value,

457
00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:51.420
we can specify that in the notify itself,

458
00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.299
and then just multiply that by our frame delta time.

459
00:17:54.299 --> 00:17:56.579
So that would be an example of like a character doing

460
00:17:56.579 --> 00:17:58.380
a sphere attack or something like that.

461
00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:00.579
So if we actually try and utilize these

462
00:18:00.579 --> 00:18:02.779
and can just quickly go through the process

463
00:18:02.779 --> 00:18:04.059
of implementing one,

464
00:18:04.059 --> 00:18:07.619
if we just went to our jump animation, for example,

465
00:18:07.619 --> 00:18:09.220
maybe I want to do our foot trace

466
00:18:09.220 --> 00:18:12.940
just as I come off the floor to kind of make it different.

467
00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:14.539
We do a different jump sound effect

468
00:18:14.539 --> 00:18:16.380
depending on what kind of floor we're on.

469
00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:17.579
So the way you add a notify

470
00:18:17.579 --> 00:18:19.940
is you have this notify tracks here.

471
00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:21.420
It's not actually in the notify here

472
00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:23.180
because you can have multiple notify tracks.

473
00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:25.740
So this one is saying this is the first notify track.

474
00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:28.420
So you simply right click here and you go to add notify.

475
00:18:28.420 --> 00:18:30.299
Now we've got add notify,

476
00:18:30.299 --> 00:18:31.660
which is our kind of play notify.

477
00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:34.019
We had add notify state,

478
00:18:34.019 --> 00:18:36.180
which is our kind of duration based one.

479
00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:38.220
So if I were to do our play notify,

480
00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:40.299
you can see we've got a couple of different options here.

481
00:18:40.299 --> 00:18:41.819
We'll come back to this in a second,

482
00:18:41.819 --> 00:18:44.500
but the ones up the top here are our custom notify.

483
00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:46.339
So these are the ones we can kind of manually

484
00:18:46.339 --> 00:18:47.180
add ourselves.

485
00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:51.180
They aren't tied to an inherited class

486
00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:52.500
in our content browser.

487
00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:56.099
These are kind of just baked into this skeleton.

488
00:18:56.099 --> 00:18:59.099
But the ones that we've specified by a class here,

489
00:18:59.099 --> 00:19:00.059
the options here,

490
00:19:00.059 --> 00:19:02.339
we can see that Unreal has already got some pre-built ones.

491
00:19:02.339 --> 00:19:05.339
So we've got the kind of standard place particles effect,

492
00:19:05.339 --> 00:19:08.140
play sound, but here's the one that we made ourselves

493
00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:09.579
is BP anim foot trace.

494
00:19:09.579 --> 00:19:11.180
So if we select that,

495
00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:13.059
we can see this is the friendly name

496
00:19:13.059 --> 00:19:14.619
that we specified through our function.

497
00:19:14.619 --> 00:19:15.539
So if I just go here,

498
00:19:15.539 --> 00:19:17.460
we can see I've called that foot trace

499
00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:20.500
and that's why the word foot trace is coming up here.

500
00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:22.299
And if I select that,

501
00:19:22.299 --> 00:19:24.900
we can actually see a couple of details

502
00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:26.579
actually about the notify itself.

503
00:19:26.579 --> 00:19:28.299
So we can specify its color.

504
00:19:28.339 --> 00:19:31.019
We can specify the type and just some additional stuff

505
00:19:31.019 --> 00:19:33.619
about whether it's got a chance to trigger

506
00:19:33.619 --> 00:19:36.160
and all these kinds of things.

507
00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:39.079
Oh, and last of all is we can see we have this,

508
00:19:39.079 --> 00:19:41.079
we made this variable public.

509
00:19:41.079 --> 00:19:43.359
And so we made this B right foot public.

510
00:19:43.359 --> 00:19:44.559
If I'd given it a category,

511
00:19:44.559 --> 00:19:47.480
it would be in a different category here,

512
00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:49.319
but as it is, it's fallen under default

513
00:19:49.319 --> 00:19:51.000
and we can see we've got the right foot.

514
00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:53.000
So we can specify which of the two feet

515
00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:54.079
we should be tracing from.

516
00:19:54.079 --> 00:19:57.000
So in this case, it would be our left,

517
00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:01.480
but if we're doing a run animation or a walk animation

518
00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:02.440
where it's gonna be alternating,

519
00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:04.960
obviously we can have multiple instances

520
00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:06.279
of this on notify

521
00:20:06.279 --> 00:20:08.039
and then we could be tracing from a different foot

522
00:20:08.039 --> 00:20:10.279
based on, we can change that property.

523
00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:12.400
Just for an example,

524
00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:14.119
if I were to then use my notify state,

525
00:20:14.119 --> 00:20:16.880
we can see if I had to do my spin attack,

526
00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:19.119
you can see this now has two key frames

527
00:20:19.119 --> 00:20:20.799
and this is kind of our duration.

528
00:20:20.799 --> 00:20:24.400
So we can see it would be firing every animation frame

529
00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:25.440
for this duration.

530
00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.119
Here we would have our begin called,

531
00:20:27.119 --> 00:20:28.279
here we would have our end called,

532
00:20:28.279 --> 00:20:30.940
pretty self-explanatory.

533
00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:32.140
The last thing that we can do,

534
00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:33.299
which we'll come back to later,

535
00:20:33.299 --> 00:20:34.819
but just whilst we're already here,

536
00:20:34.819 --> 00:20:36.500
is actually make custom notifies.

537
00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:39.660
So any existing custom notifies we have,

538
00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:41.259
we can just add here.

539
00:20:41.259 --> 00:20:42.980
We all, we can type in,

540
00:20:43.859 --> 00:20:45.900
or we can just manage our notifies and add a new one.

541
00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:49.759
So if I were just to add a custom notify,

542
00:20:49.759 --> 00:20:53.599
I can just do new notify here and just call it Bob.

543
00:20:53.599 --> 00:20:55.839
And this we'll come back to later,

544
00:20:55.839 --> 00:20:58.200
but if I wanted to do a custom notify,

545
00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:01.839
which isn't gonna have a blueprint class tied to it,

546
00:21:01.839 --> 00:21:03.160
I can just do that here.

547
00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:05.480
And I've now got Bob as an option,

548
00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:07.680
but this would need to be implemented on the anim instance,

549
00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:10.779
which we'll get to in a second.

550
00:21:10.779 --> 00:21:13.700
So that's the kind of two class-based implementations.

551
00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:16.940
But the last thing that bears mentioning

552
00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:18.900
is if I jump back to the presentation,

553
00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:21.019
you'll notice both of these functions,

554
00:21:21.019 --> 00:21:24.660
or well, really all of these functions return a bool.

555
00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:27.099
You may be wondering what that bool is actually used for.

556
00:21:27.099 --> 00:21:30.380
If you go into Unreal's C++ code implementation

557
00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:31.339
of these function calls,

558
00:21:31.339 --> 00:21:34.019
you'll see that this bool actually isn't used.

559
00:21:34.019 --> 00:21:36.819
So it's likely it's some kind of legacy use

560
00:21:36.819 --> 00:21:38.940
that's from previous versions of the engine,

561
00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:40.700
but it doesn't actually mean anything.

562
00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:42.819
So just kind of bear that in mind

563
00:21:42.819 --> 00:21:45.609
is you don't need to worry too much about that.

564
00:21:45.609 --> 00:21:47.890
To implement the other kind of anim notify,

565
00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:49.410
we're gonna have to talk a little bit more

566
00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:50.609
about the animation instance.

567
00:21:50.609 --> 00:21:52.289
So let's actually return to that.

568
00:21:52.289 --> 00:21:53.529
As we said before,

569
00:21:53.569 --> 00:21:56.690
the animation instance is broken into two key graphs,

570
00:21:56.690 --> 00:21:58.690
the event graph and the anim graph.

571
00:21:58.690 --> 00:22:01.289
The event graph is more in common

572
00:22:01.289 --> 00:22:03.289
with things you would have seen in things like Blueprints,

573
00:22:03.289 --> 00:22:06.009
other graphs you've seen in Unreal Editor.

574
00:22:06.009 --> 00:22:07.849
It's essentially responsible for reacting

575
00:22:07.849 --> 00:22:10.529
to gameplay events, getting notifies from things,

576
00:22:10.529 --> 00:22:13.289
updating the animation Blueprints idea

577
00:22:13.289 --> 00:22:14.769
of information that's going on

578
00:22:14.769 --> 00:22:17.250
outside of the animation itself.

579
00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:19.769
So this is really what gathers up the information.

580
00:22:19.769 --> 00:22:23.210
And then that information is used by the anim graph

581
00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:24.730
to drive the final pose.

582
00:22:24.730 --> 00:22:26.690
It's the anim graph that actually drives the visuals

583
00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:28.849
of that frame for how a character

584
00:22:28.849 --> 00:22:31.410
or whatever it is we're animating is gonna be posed

585
00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:33.769
and what shape its skeleton is going to be in.

586
00:22:34.650 --> 00:22:37.210
So when you're going to create a new animation instance

587
00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:38.650
or an anim blueprint,

588
00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:41.009
there's a couple of events that would be,

589
00:22:41.009 --> 00:22:42.890
first ones you would be commonly using.

590
00:22:42.890 --> 00:22:45.529
These would actually be in by default,

591
00:22:45.529 --> 00:22:48.450
but the reference we're using here is ABPMani

592
00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:49.890
from the third person template.

593
00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:51.369
So this isn't code that I've written.

594
00:22:51.369 --> 00:22:53.009
This is preexisting code.

595
00:22:53.210 --> 00:22:55.609
And we have Blueprint Initialize Animation.

596
00:22:55.609 --> 00:22:58.529
This is the equivalent of begin play from your Blueprint.

597
00:22:58.529 --> 00:23:01.450
So it's a kind of fired once right at the beginning.

598
00:23:01.450 --> 00:23:03.009
And it's where you would do initial setup

599
00:23:03.009 --> 00:23:04.250
for things like, as you can see here,

600
00:23:04.250 --> 00:23:06.609
we're caching this character reference

601
00:23:06.609 --> 00:23:07.970
just so we don't have to recast

602
00:23:07.970 --> 00:23:09.650
every time we want access to it.

603
00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:11.730
That would be a typical use.

604
00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:14.329
And then we also have the Blueprint Update Animation,

605
00:23:14.329 --> 00:23:15.890
which is a little bit more like our tick.

606
00:23:15.890 --> 00:23:17.930
So called every animation frame

607
00:23:18.890 --> 00:23:20.730
for any kind of real-time data

608
00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:23.529
that we need to update on the fly.

609
00:23:23.529 --> 00:23:27.289
So both of these have a C++ underlying them

610
00:23:27.289 --> 00:23:29.410
as with all of Unreal.

611
00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:31.329
So let's actually just take a quick look at that.

612
00:23:31.329 --> 00:23:36.329
If I open up the C++, we have the anim instance here.

613
00:23:36.490 --> 00:23:41.490
And if we go to line, I think it's 315.

614
00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:45.029
I have it off the top of my head.

615
00:23:45.029 --> 00:23:46.109
Yes.

616
00:23:46.109 --> 00:23:47.789
So here we have the kind of event

617
00:23:47.789 --> 00:23:52.789
that drives our kind of begin played like game called,

618
00:23:53.789 --> 00:23:55.269
our Blueprint Initialize Animation.

619
00:23:55.269 --> 00:23:56.109
It's actually called inside

620
00:23:56.109 --> 00:23:58.950
the Initialize Animation function in C++.

621
00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:01.309
And you can see we kind of do a little bit of setup here.

622
00:24:01.309 --> 00:24:03.670
We make sure that we've got a skeletal mesh component.

623
00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:05.829
We make sure that we've got a valid anim blueprint class

624
00:24:05.829 --> 00:24:06.950
for that skeleton.

625
00:24:06.950 --> 00:24:08.990
We calculate some bone things,

626
00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:10.390
and then we call these two events.

627
00:24:10.390 --> 00:24:12.630
We have the native initialize animation

628
00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:14.069
and the blueprint initialize animation.

629
00:24:14.069 --> 00:24:15.630
So this isn't a blueprint native event.

630
00:24:15.630 --> 00:24:18.029
It does call the C++ path first,

631
00:24:18.029 --> 00:24:21.500
and then it goes on to call the blueprint path.

632
00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:24.900
Similarly, we have something kind of similar setup

633
00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:27.460
for our tick.

634
00:24:27.460 --> 00:24:30.220
And if I just look at my notes,

635
00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:34.039
the line for that is 634.

636
00:24:34.039 --> 00:24:36.400
So we kind of have a very, very similar setup here.

637
00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:39.599
This is inside the update animation.

638
00:24:39.599 --> 00:24:42.599
This is the kind of like C++ level tick.

639
00:24:42.599 --> 00:24:45.480
And we do a little bit of something a little bit similar.

640
00:24:45.519 --> 00:24:47.759
We do some checking, checking that data is valid,

641
00:24:47.759 --> 00:24:50.359
checking that we've got a skeletal mesh component,

642
00:24:50.359 --> 00:24:52.559
updating our bones and things like this.

643
00:24:52.559 --> 00:24:55.519
And then we come to our kind of two key events.

644
00:24:55.519 --> 00:24:57.599
The kind of one thing that we wanted to highlight here

645
00:24:57.599 --> 00:24:59.359
is it's that same structure here

646
00:24:59.359 --> 00:25:01.519
where we have this native update animation.

647
00:25:01.519 --> 00:25:03.599
So if you're going to make a C++ class

648
00:25:03.599 --> 00:25:06.200
that inherits from your anim instance for any reason,

649
00:25:07.960 --> 00:25:11.119
you can always trust that it's our native C++ version

650
00:25:11.119 --> 00:25:12.200
of the function that gets called.

651
00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:15.119
And after that, we have our blueprint update animation.

652
00:25:16.089 --> 00:25:17.369
Something that's worth highlighting here,

653
00:25:17.369 --> 00:25:19.769
these are the kind of two most common events

654
00:25:19.769 --> 00:25:21.130
for us to be using.

655
00:25:21.130 --> 00:25:23.170
But if both of these will fire

656
00:25:23.170 --> 00:25:25.970
before the anim graph derives its pose.

657
00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:26.809
So like we said,

658
00:25:26.809 --> 00:25:28.329
it's kind of there to gather the data

659
00:25:28.329 --> 00:25:30.089
for the anim graph to use.

660
00:25:30.089 --> 00:25:32.210
So it makes sense that these are fired first.

661
00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:35.410
But if you need some logic that fires

662
00:25:35.410 --> 00:25:37.049
after you're certain the anim graph

663
00:25:37.049 --> 00:25:39.250
is decided on its final pose for that frame,

664
00:25:39.250 --> 00:25:42.130
there is a separate event called blueprint post evaluate

665
00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:45.769
that you can use if you need some logic to fire

666
00:25:45.769 --> 00:25:50.789
after the skeleton has defined its pose for that frame.

667
00:25:50.789 --> 00:25:52.950
Also here is, like we said,

668
00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:55.670
where we would be defining the implementations

669
00:25:55.670 --> 00:25:57.470
of our custom anim notifiers.

670
00:25:57.470 --> 00:26:00.509
So if we were to kind of like right click

671
00:26:00.509 --> 00:26:03.910
on inside the event graph of an animation blueprint

672
00:26:03.910 --> 00:26:07.109
and that the skeleton of that animation blueprint is using

673
00:26:07.109 --> 00:26:09.789
has custom notifies built into it,

674
00:26:09.789 --> 00:26:10.829
when you right click,

675
00:26:10.829 --> 00:26:14.109
you would see a list of add anim notify events like this.

676
00:26:14.109 --> 00:26:15.069
This isn't gonna show up

677
00:26:15.069 --> 00:26:17.470
for all our blueprint based anim notifiers

678
00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:18.549
because the understanding is

679
00:26:18.549 --> 00:26:20.309
they already have an implementation.

680
00:26:20.309 --> 00:26:22.789
So this is only for the custom notifiers.

681
00:26:22.789 --> 00:26:25.950
If we actually jump to our ABP Manny,

682
00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:27.750
for the most part,

683
00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:29.910
what I've been demonstrating is the kind of default,

684
00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:30.950
but I have actually added

685
00:26:30.950 --> 00:26:36.660
just a little bit of our own logic.

686
00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:38.740
So this is a notify that I've added,

687
00:26:38.740 --> 00:26:40.980
but just to kind of demonstrate that,

688
00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:43.859
if we go to our add anim notify event,

689
00:26:43.859 --> 00:26:46.059
you can see we've got a list here of all our custom ones.

690
00:26:46.059 --> 00:26:48.819
We've even got that Bob that we just added ourselves,

691
00:26:48.819 --> 00:26:50.740
just in the kind of section before,

692
00:26:50.740 --> 00:26:52.740
and we have footplant left, footplant right.

693
00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:55.740
All of these, these are actually custom anim notifiers

694
00:26:55.740 --> 00:26:57.740
that come on Manny by default,

695
00:26:57.740 --> 00:27:01.369
and Bob is obviously that one we just added.

696
00:27:01.369 --> 00:27:03.529
So as a kind of example,

697
00:27:03.529 --> 00:27:08.529
all I did here was implement what happens on footplant left.

698
00:27:08.569 --> 00:27:10.569
This naming convention isn't required of AN,

699
00:27:10.569 --> 00:27:13.329
but, you know, AN standing for anim notify, quite obviously.

700
00:27:13.329 --> 00:27:15.690
And what we're doing is a little bit like our foot trace,

701
00:27:15.690 --> 00:27:17.529
but I was just trying to demonstrate

702
00:27:17.529 --> 00:27:19.329
something slightly different.

703
00:27:19.329 --> 00:27:20.849
So on our footplant left,

704
00:27:20.849 --> 00:27:24.609
we're checking for the left socket on our skeleton

705
00:27:24.609 --> 00:27:25.609
and we're tracing downwards.

706
00:27:25.609 --> 00:27:28.170
So like our foot trace for the playing the sound effect,

707
00:27:28.170 --> 00:27:31.650
but what I've done instead is I've added a surface type

708
00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:33.809
to our physics settings of ice.

709
00:27:33.809 --> 00:27:35.130
And what I'm doing is I'm checking

710
00:27:35.130 --> 00:27:36.769
if that surface type is ice,

711
00:27:36.769 --> 00:27:38.730
then I'm rolling a random dice.

712
00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:42.450
And if that value is less than our slip chance,

713
00:27:42.490 --> 00:27:44.650
so if it's, you know, meets our slip chance,

714
00:27:44.650 --> 00:27:47.650
then I'm setting this slipping ball to true.

715
00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:50.049
And the idea being that if you're walking on ice,

716
00:27:50.049 --> 00:27:52.170
every time your foot lands on the floor,

717
00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:53.450
there's a chance you're gonna slip over

718
00:27:53.450 --> 00:27:55.809
cause you don't want to slip whilst if you're running,

719
00:27:55.809 --> 00:27:57.569
you don't want to slip whilst both your feet in the air

720
00:27:57.569 --> 00:27:58.569
cause that's gonna look a little bit silly.

721
00:27:58.569 --> 00:28:01.250
You slip as your footing is hitting the ice.

722
00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:03.250
And then we can use that

723
00:28:03.250 --> 00:28:08.519
to drive some kind of custom animations in our anim graph.

724
00:28:08.519 --> 00:28:10.319
So if you already mentioned,

725
00:28:10.319 --> 00:28:12.599
whereas the event graph is where we react

726
00:28:12.599 --> 00:28:14.400
to gameplay logic, we listen for events,

727
00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:16.119
we update our information.

728
00:28:16.119 --> 00:28:18.319
The anim graph is where we actually construct

729
00:28:18.319 --> 00:28:20.200
our final pose that our mesh is in.

730
00:28:21.480 --> 00:28:23.319
The reference we've got here in this image,

731
00:28:23.319 --> 00:28:25.079
and just I'll actually jump into the engine

732
00:28:25.079 --> 00:28:27.319
and show this, is again,

733
00:28:27.319 --> 00:28:30.839
our modified version of the Mani ABP.

734
00:28:30.839 --> 00:28:33.519
So all of this content here is what you would see

735
00:28:33.519 --> 00:28:34.680
if you opened that up,

736
00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:36.880
apart from this additional blend poses by Bool,

737
00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:38.960
which we've added in based on our slipping logic

738
00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:40.559
that we just talked about before.

739
00:28:40.559 --> 00:28:41.720
So I'll jump over to the engine actually,

740
00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:50.660
we'll go through this kind of node by node basis.

741
00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:54.460
So if we open this up in our anim graph,

742
00:28:54.460 --> 00:28:55.700
we'll see a few things.

743
00:28:56.940 --> 00:28:58.660
The kind of first thing to highlight here

744
00:28:58.660 --> 00:29:01.460
is that a little bit like the material graph

745
00:29:01.460 --> 00:29:02.819
for anyone who's used to using that

746
00:29:02.819 --> 00:29:04.539
or has some experience there,

747
00:29:04.539 --> 00:29:07.019
the anim graph works right to left.

748
00:29:07.019 --> 00:29:10.579
So this right most node is our final output

749
00:29:10.579 --> 00:29:12.900
for the kind of pose of all of our bones

750
00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:15.140
and therefore the way our character actually looks

751
00:29:15.140 --> 00:29:17.660
for that animation frame.

752
00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:19.619
And when you're talking about the logic,

753
00:29:19.619 --> 00:29:23.180
the branching that kind of goes backwards from that node.

754
00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:25.660
So the path that the node follows,

755
00:29:25.660 --> 00:29:30.059
the path that our whole code goes through

756
00:29:30.059 --> 00:29:31.859
to kind of define this final pose,

757
00:29:31.859 --> 00:29:33.579
the branching is kind of followed backwards

758
00:29:33.579 --> 00:29:36.339
from the right towards the left.

759
00:29:36.339 --> 00:29:37.980
So if we kind of take this first node

760
00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:39.259
as our example here,

761
00:29:39.259 --> 00:29:41.339
we have a blend poses by Bool node,

762
00:29:41.339 --> 00:29:44.460
and that will blend between two different paths

763
00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:46.259
depending on this Bool.

764
00:29:46.259 --> 00:29:47.099
So in this case,

765
00:29:47.099 --> 00:29:48.420
this slipping Bool that we did,

766
00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:50.180
I've used a knot here

767
00:29:50.180 --> 00:29:53.059
just because it means that I can be a little bit tidier.

768
00:29:53.059 --> 00:29:55.099
Otherwise the slipping pose would be up here

769
00:29:55.099 --> 00:29:57.460
and it'd be kind of in the way of the rest of the,

770
00:29:57.460 --> 00:29:59.380
in the way of the rest of the predefined anim graph.

771
00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:02.380
So I just thought it would kind of keep it more separate.

772
00:30:02.380 --> 00:30:03.900
But generally the concept here is pretty simple.

773
00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:04.740
If this is true,

774
00:30:04.740 --> 00:30:06.259
we would be following this path.

775
00:30:06.259 --> 00:30:08.660
And if this Bool output's false,

776
00:30:08.660 --> 00:30:10.339
then we followed this path.

777
00:30:10.339 --> 00:30:12.220
So down the false path,

778
00:30:12.220 --> 00:30:13.539
what happens is we go,

779
00:30:13.539 --> 00:30:15.900
we're just reading from our slipping state.

780
00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:17.140
This is a state machine,

781
00:30:17.140 --> 00:30:18.819
which we'll talk a little bit more about later,

782
00:30:18.819 --> 00:30:20.220
but actually this one is incredibly simple.

783
00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:22.339
It's basically just got a single animation inside of it,

784
00:30:22.339 --> 00:30:23.980
which is our slipping pose.

785
00:30:23.980 --> 00:30:25.900
The way we can actually really easily demonstrate that

786
00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:28.059
is worth talking about in itself.

787
00:30:28.059 --> 00:30:29.859
We have, if you open up your details panel,

788
00:30:29.859 --> 00:30:31.500
we have got a couple of things here.

789
00:30:31.500 --> 00:30:32.900
We have the edit defaults,

790
00:30:32.900 --> 00:30:34.819
and this is basically just all the variables

791
00:30:34.819 --> 00:30:36.220
on your anim instance,

792
00:30:36.220 --> 00:30:37.099
pretty self-explanatory,

793
00:30:37.099 --> 00:30:38.700
you define their default state.

794
00:30:40.259 --> 00:30:42.900
But in the edit selected instance,

795
00:30:42.900 --> 00:30:43.779
what you're actually doing

796
00:30:43.779 --> 00:30:45.619
is kind of editing a preview instance

797
00:30:45.619 --> 00:30:46.579
of your animation blueprint.

798
00:30:46.579 --> 00:30:48.180
I mean, you can kind of make changes there

799
00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:51.180
to just see what your anim graph would do,

800
00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:52.460
but you don't actually, you're not,

801
00:30:52.460 --> 00:30:53.740
you know, if you hit play,

802
00:30:53.740 --> 00:30:55.940
none of this information would actually get used.

803
00:30:55.940 --> 00:30:56.940
So for example, here,

804
00:30:56.940 --> 00:30:59.940
we can freely toggle between slipping and not slipping,

805
00:30:59.940 --> 00:31:02.180
and we can see what actually happens to our character.

806
00:31:02.180 --> 00:31:04.059
We can see the anim graph update,

807
00:31:04.059 --> 00:31:06.299
and we're now going down this path,

808
00:31:06.299 --> 00:31:08.700
but also you can see the final pose is being rendered.

809
00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:10.140
So this is a beautiful animation

810
00:31:10.140 --> 00:31:11.460
that I constructed myself,

811
00:31:11.500 --> 00:31:13.740
just in engine to have something

812
00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:17.180
that didn't look like the idle or the walking or whatever.

813
00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:18.980
So if we go down the true path,

814
00:31:18.980 --> 00:31:22.019
this is the section that a typical Manny ABP

815
00:31:22.019 --> 00:31:23.500
would already have in it.

816
00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:25.299
And because we're kind of not really talking

817
00:31:25.299 --> 00:31:26.900
about branching logic anymore,

818
00:31:26.900 --> 00:31:28.460
it kind of makes sense to follow this branch

819
00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:30.339
all the way down to the end.

820
00:31:30.339 --> 00:31:31.460
So where we,

821
00:31:31.460 --> 00:31:34.579
and because the reason for that being actually

822
00:31:34.579 --> 00:31:35.900
worth touching on briefly,

823
00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:40.019
is that where the branching logic is kind of right to left,

824
00:31:40.019 --> 00:31:41.259
when you're thinking about the way

825
00:31:41.779 --> 00:31:43.220
the final pose is constructed,

826
00:31:43.220 --> 00:31:45.539
the ordering of that is kind of left to right.

827
00:31:45.539 --> 00:31:49.099
So our leftmost nodes are then,

828
00:31:49.099 --> 00:31:52.059
the nodes to the right of them are applied over the top.

829
00:31:52.059 --> 00:31:53.339
So that's why it's kind of helpful

830
00:31:53.339 --> 00:31:55.619
to then go all the way to the left,

831
00:31:55.619 --> 00:31:57.819
because everything that's done after that

832
00:31:57.819 --> 00:31:59.859
is kind of additive on top of.

833
00:32:01.539 --> 00:32:03.420
So first off, we have our main state.

834
00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:04.539
This is another state machine.

835
00:32:04.539 --> 00:32:07.819
Again, we'll go into that in more detail just after this,

836
00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:11.140
but you may be wondering why this locomotion pose

837
00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:12.579
seems to be sampling at the same time.

838
00:32:12.579 --> 00:32:14.539
Like when we go into our slipping state,

839
00:32:14.539 --> 00:32:16.819
you can see it's not being used, but here we are.

840
00:32:16.819 --> 00:32:18.500
And that's just because if we quickly jump

841
00:32:18.500 --> 00:32:19.579
inside this state machine

842
00:32:19.579 --> 00:32:21.220
and go inside the locomotion state,

843
00:32:21.220 --> 00:32:24.420
you can see we're using this cached pose locomotion.

844
00:32:24.420 --> 00:32:26.539
So cached poses are essentially,

845
00:32:26.539 --> 00:32:29.420
it's a little bit like writing a function in C++

846
00:32:29.420 --> 00:32:33.180
that you write just to not have to recreate the same code,

847
00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:35.819
the same five, 10, 20 lines of code

848
00:32:35.819 --> 00:32:38.339
in five or six different places.

849
00:32:38.339 --> 00:32:40.700
Pose caching is essentially the same thing.

850
00:32:41.220 --> 00:32:42.859
If you actually try to plug in some logic

851
00:32:42.859 --> 00:32:44.660
into multiple places in the anim graph,

852
00:32:44.660 --> 00:32:45.779
it won't let you do it.

853
00:32:45.779 --> 00:32:48.339
An output pose can only go to one thing.

854
00:32:48.339 --> 00:32:52.420
So that's kind of where pose caching comes in.

855
00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:55.980
It lets us reuse this pose, whatever we create.

856
00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:57.500
In this case, we call it locomotion,

857
00:32:57.500 --> 00:33:01.980
but I could just do cached, new save cache pose.

858
00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:03.180
I could just call it whatever I want.

859
00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:04.380
I can call it Bob,

860
00:33:04.380 --> 00:33:08.140
and then you can use Bob wherever you want,

861
00:33:08.140 --> 00:33:09.859
and you can plug that in.

862
00:33:09.859 --> 00:33:12.059
You can just use that pose multiple times,

863
00:33:12.059 --> 00:33:13.779
and you can plug that into multiple places.

864
00:33:13.779 --> 00:33:15.980
And that's equivalent to plugging locomotion

865
00:33:15.980 --> 00:33:17.700
into all those places,

866
00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:20.710
or whatever logic you have going into that cache pose.

867
00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:24.710
So it's because that our main state

868
00:33:24.710 --> 00:33:26.230
uses that locomotion pose

869
00:33:26.230 --> 00:33:27.710
that we're seeing that cache pose

870
00:33:27.710 --> 00:33:29.230
be kind of like calculated

871
00:33:29.230 --> 00:33:32.150
every time we're running that main state.

872
00:33:32.150 --> 00:33:33.789
So after our main state state machine,

873
00:33:33.789 --> 00:33:34.950
again, we'll go into state machines

874
00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:36.269
a little bit more in a second.

875
00:33:36.269 --> 00:33:38.910
We've got this default slot being applied.

876
00:33:38.910 --> 00:33:40.430
Now, as we talked about a little bit

877
00:33:40.430 --> 00:33:42.750
when we looked at anim montages,

878
00:33:42.750 --> 00:33:44.390
they can play into particular slots.

879
00:33:44.390 --> 00:33:46.829
So let's just quickly jump to our jump montage,

880
00:33:46.829 --> 00:33:49.309
and ignoring the sound effect briefly,

881
00:33:49.309 --> 00:33:50.349
we can see on the left here,

882
00:33:50.349 --> 00:33:53.269
we've got all these things for our notifies

883
00:33:53.269 --> 00:33:54.109
and stuff like that,

884
00:33:54.109 --> 00:33:55.549
and we define a group,

885
00:33:55.549 --> 00:33:58.910
a slot that that anim montage plays into.

886
00:33:58.910 --> 00:34:00.069
So what that lets us do

887
00:34:00.069 --> 00:34:02.990
is inject montages that play into that slot

888
00:34:02.990 --> 00:34:05.710
at different points in our animation blueprint.

889
00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:07.869
So maybe we have an anim montage

890
00:34:07.869 --> 00:34:08.989
that only affects the hands

891
00:34:08.989 --> 00:34:10.670
because it's like a gun reload animation

892
00:34:10.670 --> 00:34:11.829
or something like that.

893
00:34:11.829 --> 00:34:13.269
And in certain situations,

894
00:34:13.269 --> 00:34:14.909
like for example, when I'm slipping,

895
00:34:14.909 --> 00:34:16.469
I don't want to allow montages

896
00:34:16.469 --> 00:34:19.150
that affect the hands to come into consideration,

897
00:34:19.150 --> 00:34:20.949
but down another path, I do.

898
00:34:20.949 --> 00:34:23.989
So that's kind of where our slots come into play

899
00:34:23.989 --> 00:34:26.469
is that this kind of lets us filter in

900
00:34:26.469 --> 00:34:29.110
and out different montages.

901
00:34:30.630 --> 00:34:32.630
Yeah, so this is where our montage would come in

902
00:34:32.630 --> 00:34:34.670
if we're playing any into the default slot.

903
00:34:34.670 --> 00:34:35.909
And then over the top of that,

904
00:34:35.909 --> 00:34:37.630
we have this control rig node.

905
00:34:37.630 --> 00:34:39.510
Control rig is a kind of advanced system

906
00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:41.670
that gives you control over the individual bones

907
00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:43.150
of a skeleton at runtime.

908
00:34:43.150 --> 00:34:44.909
We don't need to go too much into that,

909
00:34:44.909 --> 00:34:46.670
but you can kind of tell from the name here

910
00:34:46.670 --> 00:34:48.510
what this is being used for,

911
00:34:48.510 --> 00:34:50.309
which is some IK tracing.

912
00:34:50.309 --> 00:34:52.989
For anyone who's not familiar with the term IK tracing,

913
00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:55.070
IK stands for inverse kinematics,

914
00:34:55.070 --> 00:34:57.510
it's essentially giving us control

915
00:34:57.510 --> 00:35:00.510
of the end bone of a skeleton

916
00:35:00.510 --> 00:35:03.110
and defining where we want that bone to be

917
00:35:03.110 --> 00:35:04.909
and the skeleton going up the chain

918
00:35:04.909 --> 00:35:06.829
and working out where the other bones need to be

919
00:35:06.829 --> 00:35:07.949
in relation to that,

920
00:35:07.949 --> 00:35:10.269
so you get a kind of more natural pose.

921
00:35:10.269 --> 00:35:12.590
The kind of most common example when this would be used

922
00:35:12.590 --> 00:35:14.630
is when we're placing our feet on the floor,

923
00:35:14.630 --> 00:35:15.469
or say, for example,

924
00:35:15.469 --> 00:35:17.030
we have a character brushing up against a wall,

925
00:35:17.030 --> 00:35:19.150
we don't want that arm clipping through the wall,

926
00:35:19.150 --> 00:35:22.150
so we can place the hand on a surface that we detect.

927
00:35:22.150 --> 00:35:25.269
That's the kind of typical cases for IK tracing,

928
00:35:25.269 --> 00:35:26.989
if IK in general,

929
00:35:26.989 --> 00:35:28.349
but obviously there's a lot of different ways

930
00:35:28.349 --> 00:35:29.190
you can use it.

931
00:35:29.190 --> 00:35:32.269
Maybe it's your boss's head when they do a fire attack,

932
00:35:32.269 --> 00:35:33.550
you need it to follow a particular path,

933
00:35:33.550 --> 00:35:35.309
you might use IK when you don't know

934
00:35:35.309 --> 00:35:38.110
what the pose of the rest of their body is gonna be.

935
00:35:38.110 --> 00:35:39.670
So as you can see,

936
00:35:39.670 --> 00:35:42.349
you can infer from the check that we're doing here,

937
00:35:42.349 --> 00:35:43.550
this slip chance is actually,

938
00:35:43.550 --> 00:35:46.150
that's just an example where I was plugging something in.

939
00:35:48.550 --> 00:35:50.869
We're basically performing this IK trace

940
00:35:50.869 --> 00:35:52.349
when we're not falling.

941
00:35:52.349 --> 00:35:53.989
So is falling is the ball

942
00:35:53.989 --> 00:35:55.710
that comes from our character movement component

943
00:35:55.710 --> 00:35:57.230
is when we're in the air,

944
00:35:57.230 --> 00:36:00.829
so we're not doing our IK tracing

945
00:36:00.829 --> 00:36:02.789
when we're in the air.

946
00:36:02.789 --> 00:36:04.269
Obviously, if we jumped up into the air,

947
00:36:04.269 --> 00:36:05.590
we don't want our feet

948
00:36:05.590 --> 00:36:07.469
to stay magnetically attached to the floor,

949
00:36:07.469 --> 00:36:08.710
we want them just to take on

950
00:36:08.710 --> 00:36:10.670
our whatever jump pose we're doing.

951
00:36:10.670 --> 00:36:13.750
So we are doing the IK tracing when we're on the floor

952
00:36:13.750 --> 00:36:17.039
and we're not doing it when we are in the air.

953
00:36:17.039 --> 00:36:18.760
And so that's how I kind of pose layers up.

954
00:36:18.760 --> 00:36:19.719
We have the state machine,

955
00:36:19.719 --> 00:36:21.559
then we apply any montages

956
00:36:21.559 --> 00:36:23.119
playing into the default slot over the top of that.

957
00:36:23.119 --> 00:36:24.960
And finally, over the top of that,

958
00:36:24.960 --> 00:36:26.400
we're doing our IK,

959
00:36:26.400 --> 00:36:27.960
and that ordering is quite important.

960
00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:29.659
We don't want our IK to be done

961
00:36:29.659 --> 00:36:31.760
before our montage slot, for example,

962
00:36:31.760 --> 00:36:34.480
because if our montage affected our feet in some way,

963
00:36:34.480 --> 00:36:36.119
suddenly our feet will be coming off the floor,

964
00:36:36.119 --> 00:36:37.199
which kind of defeats the point

965
00:36:37.199 --> 00:36:39.639
of doing the IK in the first place.

966
00:36:39.639 --> 00:36:43.280
So that's the actual general structure of this anim graph.

967
00:36:43.280 --> 00:36:44.679
It's relatively small.

968
00:36:44.679 --> 00:36:47.679
If you were to see a full anim graph from a shipped game,

969
00:36:47.679 --> 00:36:49.679
it would likely be a lot more substantial than this.

970
00:36:49.679 --> 00:36:52.400
This is just a kind of a good example.

971
00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:53.880
So talking a little bit more

972
00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:58.159
about the broader concepts of the anim graph,

973
00:36:58.159 --> 00:36:59.360
one of the key things to bear in mind

974
00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:00.880
is there's a reason the anim graph

975
00:37:00.880 --> 00:37:02.719
is distinct from the event graph.

976
00:37:02.719 --> 00:37:04.719
And one of the things that that lets us do

977
00:37:04.719 --> 00:37:06.679
is unlike the event graph,

978
00:37:06.679 --> 00:37:09.500
the anim graph can make use of multi-threading.

979
00:37:09.500 --> 00:37:11.280
That is enabled by default,

980
00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:12.739
but the way you would toggle that

981
00:37:12.739 --> 00:37:15.400
is if you go to your class settings,

982
00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:20.030
if we look up, use multi-threaded animation update.

983
00:37:20.030 --> 00:37:23.230
So as you can see by default, this is on anyway.

984
00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:25.809
And what this just kind of does what it says on the tin,

985
00:37:25.809 --> 00:37:26.969
obviously with an anim graph,

986
00:37:26.969 --> 00:37:28.849
you might be doing a lot of operations

987
00:37:28.849 --> 00:37:30.690
and calculations on bones.

988
00:37:30.690 --> 00:37:32.690
It could get quite expensive

989
00:37:32.690 --> 00:37:36.570
and particularly when you have tens, possibly hundreds

990
00:37:36.570 --> 00:37:38.110
of characters on your screen,

991
00:37:39.010 --> 00:37:40.530
being able to break that up

992
00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:43.250
so that we can make use of multi-core architecture

993
00:37:43.250 --> 00:37:45.909
is obviously gonna be relatively useful.

994
00:37:45.909 --> 00:37:47.469
So that's enabled by default,

995
00:37:47.469 --> 00:37:48.769
but that doesn't necessarily mean

996
00:37:48.769 --> 00:37:52.570
that that multi-threadedness can necessarily be utilized

997
00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:56.190
if we're doing certain things improperly.

998
00:37:56.190 --> 00:37:59.269
So what that means is there's only a certain number

999
00:37:59.269 --> 00:38:01.030
of operations that we can actually perform

1000
00:38:01.030 --> 00:38:02.750
on our anim graph safely.

1001
00:38:03.269 --> 00:38:05.389
One of those is just getting values.

1002
00:38:05.389 --> 00:38:06.989
We can be relatively sure that those values

1003
00:38:06.989 --> 00:38:09.590
aren't going to change mid anim graph tick.

1004
00:38:10.750 --> 00:38:13.309
So that would be grabbing any of these variables

1005
00:38:13.309 --> 00:38:15.150
that we have just defined from our event graph.

1006
00:38:15.150 --> 00:38:16.750
We know that our event graph is run first

1007
00:38:16.750 --> 00:38:18.829
and then our anim graph runs after that.

1008
00:38:18.829 --> 00:38:20.550
So if that's broken up into multiple threads,

1009
00:38:20.550 --> 00:38:22.670
we can be relatively certain that those values

1010
00:38:22.670 --> 00:38:26.010
aren't gonna change during those anim graph threads firing.

1011
00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:29.280
We can also perform any,

1012
00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:31.320
well, we can also perform any pure functions

1013
00:38:31.320 --> 00:38:32.280
on the anim graph,

1014
00:38:32.280 --> 00:38:34.239
but it's worth talking about something here.

1015
00:38:34.239 --> 00:38:37.320
So if we have a function here

1016
00:38:37.320 --> 00:38:41.119
and we were to mark that function as pure,

1017
00:38:41.119 --> 00:38:43.280
it can get used on the anim graph.

1018
00:38:43.280 --> 00:38:45.239
But if we untick pure,

1019
00:38:45.239 --> 00:38:47.519
so that's just like a normal function

1020
00:38:47.519 --> 00:38:49.239
that you would see in your event graph.

1021
00:38:49.239 --> 00:38:52.400
If I were to call this sum return function here,

1022
00:38:52.400 --> 00:38:53.800
we'd see that's what a normal

1023
00:38:53.800 --> 00:38:55.159
on real function looks like.

1024
00:38:55.159 --> 00:38:57.760
Again, this is covered in our previous talk,

1025
00:38:57.760 --> 00:38:59.480
but if we change that to a pure function,

1026
00:38:59.480 --> 00:39:00.320
it would look like this.

1027
00:39:00.320 --> 00:39:02.719
So the function is kind of run

1028
00:39:02.719 --> 00:39:05.539
as we try to get the output of the function.

1029
00:39:05.539 --> 00:39:06.960
It's only pure functions

1030
00:39:06.960 --> 00:39:08.599
that can actually be used on our anim graph.

1031
00:39:08.599 --> 00:39:11.360
So if we put that here, that works fine.

1032
00:39:11.360 --> 00:39:17.019
If we change that from being a pure function,

1033
00:39:17.019 --> 00:39:18.900
suddenly it won't let us use it on the graph.

1034
00:39:18.900 --> 00:39:19.980
So for a start,

1035
00:39:19.980 --> 00:39:22.699
functions have to be pure to be used on the anim graph.

1036
00:39:22.699 --> 00:39:24.579
But on top of that,

1037
00:39:24.579 --> 00:39:25.440
those functions,

1038
00:39:25.440 --> 00:39:29.059
if we want to utilize that multi-threading properly,

1039
00:39:29.059 --> 00:39:30.780
we need to make sure that those functions

1040
00:39:30.820 --> 00:39:33.619
aren't doing something that wouldn't be thread safe.

1041
00:39:33.619 --> 00:39:35.820
So if I actually put that here,

1042
00:39:35.820 --> 00:39:38.219
and there's a setting that we can use on our,

1043
00:39:39.219 --> 00:39:41.059
in fact, maybe I would default it'll actually do this.

1044
00:39:41.059 --> 00:39:43.219
If I plug this in here,

1045
00:39:43.219 --> 00:39:45.860
it's gonna basically generate a warning for us

1046
00:39:45.860 --> 00:39:49.340
saying that essentially this function isn't safe,

1047
00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:54.179
that we're using potentially thread unsafe call.

1048
00:39:54.179 --> 00:39:55.699
So the way we would stop that

1049
00:39:55.699 --> 00:39:56.980
kind of complaining about this,

1050
00:39:56.980 --> 00:39:57.820
it would say,

1051
00:39:57.820 --> 00:39:59.380
well, specifically that we're gonna make sure

1052
00:39:59.380 --> 00:40:01.139
that this function is thread safe,

1053
00:40:01.900 --> 00:40:04.840
which we just do by tagging this flag here

1054
00:40:04.840 --> 00:40:06.599
and making that function thread safe.

1055
00:40:06.599 --> 00:40:11.400
But what that actually means is that our,

1056
00:40:11.400 --> 00:40:13.119
by marking a function as thread safe,

1057
00:40:13.119 --> 00:40:15.619
it kind of limits the functions that we can call by that.

1058
00:40:15.619 --> 00:40:17.320
When you say a function is thread safe,

1059
00:40:17.320 --> 00:40:19.239
that function in turn can only call

1060
00:40:19.239 --> 00:40:20.719
other thread safe functions.

1061
00:40:20.719 --> 00:40:21.599
So there is,

1062
00:40:21.599 --> 00:40:22.800
it's not just a free thing.

1063
00:40:22.800 --> 00:40:24.239
You don't just take that flag

1064
00:40:24.239 --> 00:40:26.519
and then you get all that functionality for free.

1065
00:40:26.519 --> 00:40:28.000
It does have certain implications about

1066
00:40:28.000 --> 00:40:31.179
what you can and cannot do in that function.

1067
00:40:31.219 --> 00:40:33.619
The other thing to be aware of for optimization sake

1068
00:40:33.619 --> 00:40:36.699
is the idea of AnimFastPath.

1069
00:40:36.699 --> 00:40:38.539
This used to be a setting in your project settings

1070
00:40:38.539 --> 00:40:39.380
that you would have to enable,

1071
00:40:39.380 --> 00:40:40.420
but again, it's on by default

1072
00:40:40.420 --> 00:40:42.099
and I don't think you can actually even,

1073
00:40:42.099 --> 00:40:43.280
I don't think it's actually available

1074
00:40:43.280 --> 00:40:44.780
in the project settings anymore,

1075
00:40:44.780 --> 00:40:46.019
but you'll notice on the AnimGraph,

1076
00:40:46.019 --> 00:40:48.300
we have these lightning bolt icons.

1077
00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:50.460
That means that this is utilizing, as it says,

1078
00:40:50.460 --> 00:40:51.380
FastPath enabled.

1079
00:40:51.380 --> 00:40:53.539
This node is not using any blueprint calls

1080
00:40:53.539 --> 00:40:54.699
to update its data.

1081
00:40:54.699 --> 00:40:58.019
And that is pretty much encapsulates

1082
00:40:58.019 --> 00:40:59.699
exactly what FastPath does.

1083
00:40:59.739 --> 00:41:02.539
FastPath is when you are not using

1084
00:41:02.539 --> 00:41:04.940
the blueprint virtual machine in any way

1085
00:41:04.940 --> 00:41:06.219
to feed into your data.

1086
00:41:06.219 --> 00:41:07.920
Because as soon as you're using

1087
00:41:07.920 --> 00:41:10.059
the kind of blueprint logic end of things,

1088
00:41:10.059 --> 00:41:11.480
it's going to slow things down.

1089
00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:13.199
So the point of FastPath is basically

1090
00:41:13.199 --> 00:41:14.900
just hooking into native code

1091
00:41:14.900 --> 00:41:16.460
and we're not using anything else

1092
00:41:16.460 --> 00:41:18.869
that might slow the AnimGraph down.

1093
00:41:18.869 --> 00:41:22.469
If I were to plug some value in,

1094
00:41:22.469 --> 00:41:24.550
even if it's just a float that we have

1095
00:41:24.550 --> 00:41:28.099
on our AnimGraph, you can see

1096
00:41:28.099 --> 00:41:29.739
that that's going to be okay with no complaints.

1097
00:41:29.739 --> 00:41:31.059
We've still got our lightning bolt.

1098
00:41:31.739 --> 00:41:34.139
As soon as I do even some logic to this float,

1099
00:41:34.139 --> 00:41:36.539
so just a multiply, even if I multiply it by one,

1100
00:41:36.539 --> 00:41:38.900
so it's going to end up as exactly the same value,

1101
00:41:38.900 --> 00:41:41.460
we suddenly lose our lightning bolt.

1102
00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:43.539
And that's because this is no longer using FastPath

1103
00:41:43.539 --> 00:41:45.579
because we're doing some blueprint logic

1104
00:41:45.579 --> 00:41:47.019
to feed into this value.

1105
00:41:47.019 --> 00:41:48.420
We should be trying to minimize

1106
00:41:48.420 --> 00:41:51.420
and have preferably absolutely no blueprint logic

1107
00:41:51.420 --> 00:41:52.420
in our AnimGraph.

1108
00:41:52.420 --> 00:41:54.880
Anything that is going to feed into that value,

1109
00:41:54.880 --> 00:41:56.619
it's better if we do it in the event graph

1110
00:41:56.619 --> 00:41:59.440
and then just read that data into our AnimGraph.

1111
00:42:00.480 --> 00:42:01.400
As you can already see,

1112
00:42:01.400 --> 00:42:02.920
when we're doing things like that,

1113
00:42:02.920 --> 00:42:04.519
we are occasionally getting warnings

1114
00:42:04.519 --> 00:42:05.960
and that's because I've got this setting here

1115
00:42:05.960 --> 00:42:07.940
which won't come turned on by default,

1116
00:42:07.940 --> 00:42:10.119
which is warn about blueprint usage.

1117
00:42:10.119 --> 00:42:12.039
So if we have that turned on,

1118
00:42:12.039 --> 00:42:15.010
then we'll see we get warnings.

1119
00:42:15.010 --> 00:42:17.800
I don't think that's turned on by default.

1120
00:42:17.800 --> 00:42:19.079
So we can see we get a warning

1121
00:42:19.079 --> 00:42:21.239
that we're essentially doing some blueprint logic

1122
00:42:21.239 --> 00:42:22.760
to feed into this value.

1123
00:42:22.760 --> 00:42:24.059
And that's just a really helpful thing

1124
00:42:24.059 --> 00:42:24.900
to kind of remind you.

1125
00:42:24.900 --> 00:42:26.719
But if you kind of know what you're doing there

1126
00:42:26.719 --> 00:42:27.760
and you're doing that consciously

1127
00:42:27.760 --> 00:42:29.639
and you just don't want these annoying warnings around,

1128
00:42:29.639 --> 00:42:31.760
you can just turn warn about blueprint usage

1129
00:42:31.760 --> 00:42:33.119
off on your class settings

1130
00:42:33.119 --> 00:42:35.340
and that's not going to complain in the same way.

1131
00:42:35.340 --> 00:42:36.559
The logic is still happening,

1132
00:42:36.559 --> 00:42:40.039
but it's just not going to warn you.

1133
00:42:40.039 --> 00:42:42.820
So that about covers it for the AnimGraph in general.

1134
00:42:42.820 --> 00:42:48.699
Next, let's look at state machines.

1135
00:42:48.699 --> 00:42:51.440
So this is an example of our state machine

1136
00:42:51.440 --> 00:42:56.320
that is present in this same animated AnimAVP.

1137
00:42:56.320 --> 00:42:57.400
This actually doesn't have

1138
00:42:57.400 --> 00:42:59.079
any additional logic on top of it.

1139
00:42:59.079 --> 00:43:00.320
So if we just go inside here,

1140
00:43:00.320 --> 00:43:04.719
we can see this is our land jump fall loop.

1141
00:43:04.719 --> 00:43:09.739
And if we go inside our locomotion state machine,

1142
00:43:09.739 --> 00:43:11.980
we can see this is a locomotion state machine

1143
00:43:11.980 --> 00:43:13.099
that we've demonstrated there.

1144
00:43:13.099 --> 00:43:14.980
So I'm just going to talk about, use the images now

1145
00:43:14.980 --> 00:43:17.579
just because it's kind of all in one place.

1146
00:43:17.579 --> 00:43:19.579
But the way the state machine works,

1147
00:43:19.579 --> 00:43:20.960
the state machine is not a concept

1148
00:43:20.960 --> 00:43:23.460
that is unique to animation in Unreal.

1149
00:43:23.460 --> 00:43:24.900
It's a broad concept.

1150
00:43:24.900 --> 00:43:28.340
It's the idea that you have a system

1151
00:43:28.340 --> 00:43:30.340
that can be in one state at a time

1152
00:43:30.340 --> 00:43:32.539
and these states define

1153
00:43:32.539 --> 00:43:34.820
how they might transition to each other.

1154
00:43:34.820 --> 00:43:36.780
It can sometimes be used for game screens

1155
00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:38.579
and plenty other bits of logic,

1156
00:43:38.579 --> 00:43:40.440
but in the context of the AnimGraph,

1157
00:43:40.440 --> 00:43:42.840
they have their own bespoke implementation.

1158
00:43:42.840 --> 00:43:47.019
And essentially a state can be represented by just,

1159
00:43:47.019 --> 00:43:50.059
quite typically it will be represented by just an animation,

1160
00:43:50.059 --> 00:43:51.659
but you can actually go inside a state

1161
00:43:51.659 --> 00:43:54.460
and have whole other sequences of logic in here.

1162
00:43:54.460 --> 00:43:56.420
You can have a whole bunch of nodes feeding in

1163
00:43:56.420 --> 00:43:58.900
that define what actually goes into this state.

1164
00:43:58.900 --> 00:44:00.639
You can actually have a state machine

1165
00:44:00.639 --> 00:44:03.179
that feeds inside the state of another state machine.

1166
00:44:03.179 --> 00:44:05.940
So you can go pretty, pretty far down the rabbit hole here

1167
00:44:05.940 --> 00:44:08.860
but in this case and in majority of cases,

1168
00:44:08.860 --> 00:44:11.260
it wouldn't be surprising if the majority of states

1169
00:44:11.260 --> 00:44:14.760
that you see are just represented by an animation.

1170
00:44:14.760 --> 00:44:17.099
And so the way that these states transition

1171
00:44:17.099 --> 00:44:20.139
from one state to another is defined by these rules.

1172
00:44:20.139 --> 00:44:23.460
When you would actually manually specify

1173
00:44:23.460 --> 00:44:26.119
that a state can transition to another state.

1174
00:44:26.119 --> 00:44:29.360
If I were to add one to our state machine here,

1175
00:44:29.360 --> 00:44:31.119
I could just call this state whatever,

1176
00:44:31.119 --> 00:44:33.469
I'll just call it Alan.

1177
00:44:33.469 --> 00:44:35.869
And by default, this state isn't entered into.

1178
00:44:36.750 --> 00:44:39.949
If I wanted to be able to transition into a state

1179
00:44:39.949 --> 00:44:42.610
from another one, you would just simply hover

1180
00:44:42.610 --> 00:44:45.250
over that state, click and drag and connect that up.

1181
00:44:45.250 --> 00:44:47.210
And what this is essentially saying

1182
00:44:47.210 --> 00:44:50.210
is that we can transition from this state to this state

1183
00:44:50.210 --> 00:44:52.670
when this rule is true.

1184
00:44:53.829 --> 00:44:55.449
And that has very particular meanings

1185
00:44:55.449 --> 00:44:57.570
that is a one-way transition.

1186
00:44:57.570 --> 00:45:01.409
So we can see here that idle can transition to walk run

1187
00:45:01.409 --> 00:45:03.710
and walk run can transition back to idle.

1188
00:45:03.710 --> 00:45:05.489
That is not the case here to Alan.

1189
00:45:05.489 --> 00:45:08.130
Alan cannot transition back to walk run

1190
00:45:08.130 --> 00:45:11.449
unless we go to Alan and we manually define that

1191
00:45:11.449 --> 00:45:14.489
in which case this is a two-way transition.

1192
00:45:14.489 --> 00:45:16.489
So that's already implemented as an example

1193
00:45:16.489 --> 00:45:18.570
that is already implemented in our main states.

1194
00:45:18.570 --> 00:45:21.349
We can see here, this is made a little bit more confusing

1195
00:45:21.349 --> 00:45:23.530
by the fact that we're using state aliases

1196
00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:25.150
which I'll touch on in a second.

1197
00:45:25.150 --> 00:45:27.349
But if we just park that for a second,

1198
00:45:27.349 --> 00:45:29.710
we can see that from our two falling state,

1199
00:45:29.710 --> 00:45:31.150
we can transition into jump

1200
00:45:31.150 --> 00:45:33.469
but jump can't go back to our two falling,

1201
00:45:33.469 --> 00:45:36.110
our for loop can't transition back to jump.

1202
00:45:36.110 --> 00:45:40.679
So this flow only goes this way, okay?

1203
00:45:40.679 --> 00:45:43.960
If we were to actually jump into one of these transitions

1204
00:45:43.960 --> 00:45:46.239
we can kind of look at what that might look like.

1205
00:45:46.239 --> 00:45:48.360
It's essentially just a ball that says,

1206
00:45:48.360 --> 00:45:49.960
can we make this transition?

1207
00:45:49.960 --> 00:45:52.940
And this is getting asked all the time we're in a state.

1208
00:45:52.940 --> 00:45:56.300
So whenever we are in any state,

1209
00:45:56.300 --> 00:45:59.940
so if we just jump into our idle state, for example,

1210
00:45:59.940 --> 00:46:02.460
it is cut every frame it is asking,

1211
00:46:02.460 --> 00:46:03.780
is looking at all the states

1212
00:46:03.780 --> 00:46:06.579
that the idle state can transition to.

1213
00:46:06.579 --> 00:46:09.280
And it's basically querying what this rule is.

1214
00:46:09.280 --> 00:46:11.119
So in this example, we have,

1215
00:46:11.119 --> 00:46:13.539
it's just asking whether should move is true.

1216
00:46:13.539 --> 00:46:14.820
So if that were true,

1217
00:46:14.820 --> 00:46:16.500
then we would transition to walk run.

1218
00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:17.699
And then at that point,

1219
00:46:17.699 --> 00:46:19.320
we would be in the walk run state

1220
00:46:19.320 --> 00:46:21.880
and it would be constantly asking,

1221
00:46:21.880 --> 00:46:24.840
querying all the transitions that we can make from walk run.

1222
00:46:24.840 --> 00:46:27.099
And in this case, it would be back to idle.

1223
00:46:27.099 --> 00:46:30.920
And the question it's asking is,

1224
00:46:30.920 --> 00:46:33.800
should move not true, okay?

1225
00:46:34.940 --> 00:46:37.239
So if we were just kind of given an example of that,

1226
00:46:37.239 --> 00:46:39.780
I don't know where should move is,

1227
00:46:39.780 --> 00:46:43.309
but if we look it up, yeah.

1228
00:46:43.309 --> 00:46:45.030
If we were just to set that to true,

1229
00:46:45.030 --> 00:46:48.349
we can see instantly it would transition to walk run.

1230
00:46:48.349 --> 00:46:50.190
And if I turn that off again,

1231
00:46:50.190 --> 00:46:51.190
then it transitions back

1232
00:46:51.190 --> 00:46:54.469
and you can actually see that transition firing, okay?

1233
00:46:54.469 --> 00:46:56.869
But each state can actually transition

1234
00:46:56.869 --> 00:46:59.309
to multiple different states if you so desire.

1235
00:46:59.309 --> 00:47:01.510
It doesn't have to just be a one-to-one relationship.

1236
00:47:01.510 --> 00:47:06.510
So if we were to take this example here,

1237
00:47:06.590 --> 00:47:08.869
we've used, again, state aliases have been used here

1238
00:47:08.869 --> 00:47:10.269
to kind of tidy this up.

1239
00:47:10.269 --> 00:47:11.630
But actually what is happening here,

1240
00:47:11.630 --> 00:47:14.110
if we kind of like remove these state aliases

1241
00:47:14.110 --> 00:47:16.980
just for a second to kind of simplify this,

1242
00:47:16.980 --> 00:47:19.460
what we've actually said is,

1243
00:47:19.460 --> 00:47:21.119
if I were just to quickly look at those rules,

1244
00:47:22.079 --> 00:47:25.639
if we took the same rule and applied it here,

1245
00:47:25.639 --> 00:47:28.320
so I'm just gonna quickly delete these,

1246
00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:33.269
I could connect land to jump and land to fall loop

1247
00:47:33.269 --> 00:47:35.070
using those same rules, right?

1248
00:47:35.070 --> 00:47:36.969
So clearly if I'm falling

1249
00:47:36.969 --> 00:47:39.429
and our velocity is greater than zero,

1250
00:47:39.429 --> 00:47:42.989
then I should be going into fall into our jump state.

1251
00:47:42.989 --> 00:47:50.840
And if I just quickly go back

1252
00:47:50.840 --> 00:47:58.210
and look at what the transition rule was before.

1253
00:47:58.929 --> 00:48:00.489
Okay, so it's just going into our fall loop

1254
00:48:00.489 --> 00:48:02.610
if we don't have any upwards velocity.

1255
00:48:02.610 --> 00:48:07.599
So if I would just repeat that,

1256
00:48:07.599 --> 00:48:10.630
that would be doing exactly the same thing.

1257
00:48:10.630 --> 00:48:16.239
So just the same thing, but without that logic there.

1258
00:48:16.239 --> 00:48:20.539
So if we are falling and we have upwards velocity,

1259
00:48:20.539 --> 00:48:21.820
we would transition to jump.

1260
00:48:21.820 --> 00:48:24.059
And if we're falling and we don't have upwards velocity,

1261
00:48:24.059 --> 00:48:27.280
then we would transition straight into our fall loop.

1262
00:48:27.280 --> 00:48:29.980
What the state aliases allow us to do

1263
00:48:30.019 --> 00:48:33.260
is just prevent having so many lines.

1264
00:48:33.260 --> 00:48:35.880
If we wanna be able to do those exact same queries

1265
00:48:35.880 --> 00:48:37.260
for our locomotion to jump

1266
00:48:37.260 --> 00:48:38.820
and our locomotion to fall loop,

1267
00:48:38.820 --> 00:48:41.219
you can see we end up with a pretty complicated graph.

1268
00:48:41.219 --> 00:48:42.059
And then, you know,

1269
00:48:42.059 --> 00:48:44.219
fall loop needs to be able to transition to land

1270
00:48:44.219 --> 00:48:46.260
and jump needs to be able to transition to land.

1271
00:48:46.260 --> 00:48:49.380
You end up with this like incredibly verbose

1272
00:48:49.380 --> 00:48:52.019
and complicated looking anim graph.

1273
00:48:52.019 --> 00:48:53.980
What the state aliases let us do

1274
00:48:53.980 --> 00:48:58.019
is kind of describe exactly the same thing.

1275
00:48:58.019 --> 00:49:02.460
I can just undo back.

1276
00:49:02.460 --> 00:49:05.860
But they essentially say, you put in the state alias.

1277
00:49:05.860 --> 00:49:08.739
So if I would just add one here and you just say,

1278
00:49:08.739 --> 00:49:12.099
which, if I just drag this down here,

1279
00:49:12.099 --> 00:49:15.719
you just say, which states is this kind of a shortcut for?

1280
00:49:15.719 --> 00:49:18.380
So the two falling is kind of acting,

1281
00:49:18.380 --> 00:49:21.539
the rules it's applying are rules that are applied

1282
00:49:21.539 --> 00:49:23.420
to both the locomotion and land,

1283
00:49:23.420 --> 00:49:25.780
meaning that both locomotion and land

1284
00:49:25.780 --> 00:49:28.340
can transition to jump using this rule.

1285
00:49:28.340 --> 00:49:31.199
And they can both transition to fall loop using this rule.

1286
00:49:31.360 --> 00:49:32.679
And it's just the same in reverse,

1287
00:49:32.679 --> 00:49:36.079
to land is applying the rules for both jump and fall loop,

1288
00:49:36.079 --> 00:49:39.239
meaning jump can transition to land using this rule

1289
00:49:39.239 --> 00:49:41.139
and fall loop can transition to land

1290
00:49:41.139 --> 00:49:42.869
using this rule as well.

1291
00:49:42.869 --> 00:49:44.750
So the state aliases are just kind of there

1292
00:49:44.750 --> 00:49:47.190
to make that a bit visually easier on the eye

1293
00:49:47.190 --> 00:49:49.230
and understand how that flow works.

1294
00:49:49.230 --> 00:49:51.110
But ultimately the logic being executed

1295
00:49:51.110 --> 00:49:52.289
is exactly the same.
